# Rust build artifacts
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Crash reports
*.rs~
*.swp
.*.swp

# Local environment and secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Editor / OS noise
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Python helper scripts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Synthesis output used to be written relative to the process working directory,
# so running the tests dropped a manifest here per synthesis. It now goes to
# `.unia/out` and both the transducer and the registry default to that location,
# so the corpus measured locally is the corpus a cloner gets. See SPEC.md §6.

# Belt and braces. Synthesis output no longer lands here — it goes to `.unia/out`
# — so this only catches droppings from an older checkout or a caller that set
# UNIA_OUT_DIR to the repository root.
/*.ure

# Synthesised manifests now land here rather than in the working directory, and
# the store still walks them, so a synthesised actuator remains part of the
# corpus. Untracked for the same reason as the root droppings it replaces.
/.unia/

# Provenance artefact for the prior EUNIA submission (docs/proof/).
#
# NOT committed: the rendered Devpost page contains the author's civil name, and
# this repository is public. Committing it would publish that name inside the
# repository, which defeats the pseudonym the paper uses. The paper cites the
# canonical URL instead, which any interested reader can follow themselves.
#
# Regenerate with:
#   chromium --headless --no-sandbox \
#     --user-agent="Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) Chrome/148.0.0.0" \
#     --virtual-time-budget=20000 --print-to-pdf=docs/proof/devpost-eunia-2020.pdf \
#     "https://devpost.com/software/eunia-european-union-national-informal-assistance-oz4kcp"
docs/proof/*.pdf

# LaTeX build intermediates (the .pdf is tracked, the byproducts are not)
docs/whitepaper/*.aux
docs/whitepaper/*.log
docs/whitepaper/*.out
